  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates an optical amplification scheme that combines a multipass cell with a lithium niobate crystal. A weak signal pulse and a stronger pump pulse enter the setup together and are directed into a cavity formed by coated mirrors. Inside this multipass cell, the beam is reflected back and forth several times, so a single entry produces multiple traversals through the nonlinear crystal region. The repeated crossings increase the interaction length between pump and signal, leading to stronger amplification.\n\nThe colored pulse sketches show the key change: at the input, the pump is much larger than the signal, while at the output the signal has grown substantially after passing through the cell. The central inset emphasizes the temporal behavior, indicating a group delay between the pump and the amplified signal, with a smaller idler pulse also produced. Near the crystal, the diagram labels the generated idler as another output of the nonlinear interaction.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that recycling the beams through a compact mirror arrangement boosts parametric amplification in lithium niobate, enhancing the signal efficiently while also generating an idler component.",
